stext: keep RTL clusters in logical order

LibreOffice exports each Arabic letter with its diacritics as an
ActualText, in logical order, while the glyphs around it are drawn in
visual order. The mark glyph's own unicode is bogus ('B', 'U'), so it
got bidi level 0 and split the RTL run, and reverse_bidi_line() then
reversed the cluster's chars with the run. A glyph whose unicode is
several chars (the lam-alef ligature "لا") was reversed the same way.

Chars after the first of one glyph's unicode or of one ActualText are
flagged as continuing a cluster; reversing a run keeps each cluster in
order. An RTL ActualText's chars get an RTL bidi level, add no synthetic
spaces (its first char sits at the mark glyph, offset from the base) and
a base glyph carrying a mark still advances the pen.

Also: the glyphs can spell a whole ActualText and go on (assert), and
the postfix flush counted bytes, not runes, sending a multi-byte postfix
twice. (SumatraPDF #6485)

diff --git a/source/fitz/stext-device.c b/source/fitz/stext-device.c
